NAME
       column - line up text in columns

SYNOPSIS
       column -t [-s SEPARATORS] [-o OUTPUT-SEPARATOR] [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
       With -t, column reads lines, splits each into fields and prints them
       as a table with every column as wide as its longest entry. Fields are
       split at white space unless -s names other characters. It makes files
       like /etc/passwd or a CSV much easier to read.

OPTIONS
       -t, --table
              Print a table.

       -s CHARS, --separator CHARS
              Split fields at these characters, for example -s : or -s ,.

       -o TEXT, --output-separator TEXT
              Put TEXT between columns (two spaces by default).

EXAMPLES
       Show /etc/passwd as a table:

           column -t -s : /etc/passwd

       Line up the output of mount:

           mount | column -t

SEE ALSO
       cut(1), awk(1), paste(1)
